> app.use(express.json({ limit: '10mb' })); // Protect against huge payloads
> 
> /* ====== RATE LIMITING ====== */
> const limiter = rateLimit({
>   windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,      // 15 minutes
>   max: 100,                      // 100 requests per window per IP
>   standardHeaders: true,
>   legacyHeaders: false,
> });
> app.use(limiter);
> 
> /* ====== ENDPOINTS ====== */
> // Your existing routes below…
> ```

---

## 2. Input/Output Handling

| Issue | Why ❌ | Recommended Fix 💡 |
|-------|--------|--------------------|
| **No input validation** | Even for `/users`, future query parameters could be added; unvalidated user data can lead to enumeration or injection. | Use `express-validator` or another validation library for any query/param/​body data. |
| **Potential enumeration via `/users`** | The endpoint exposes a list of all user data for every request (sensitive emails, IDs). | Move it under authentication or throttle, or expose only the fields needed. |
| **No content‑type handling** | A bad client could send malformed JSON and crash the server. | The `express.json()` middleware with a payload limit protects, but double‑check parsing errors. |

> **Code Example (validating query params for /health)**  
> ```js
> const { query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');
> 
> app.get(
>   '/health',
>   query('tz').optional().isString().trim().escape(),
>   (req, res, next) => {
>     const errors = validationResult(req);
>     if (!errors.isEmpty()) return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
>     // Normal logic
>     res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
>   }
> );
> ```

---

## 3. Error Management

| Issue | Why ❌ | Recommended Fix 💡 |
|-------|--------|--------------------|
| **No global error handler** | Uncaught errors crash the process; leaked stack traces give attackers clues. | Add an Express error‑handling middleware and capture all unhandled promise rejections. |
| **Missing 404 handler** | A 404 can reveal that routes are missing – not a big risk, but good practice. | Add a middleware after all routes that returns a friendly JSON 404. |
| **Missing `process.on('unhandledRejection')`** | Promises that reject without a `.catch()` will silently terminate the server or expose stack traces. | Graceful termination + logging. |

> **Error handling snippet**  
> ```js
> /* ====== 404 handler ====== */
> app.use((req, res, next) => {
>   res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not Found' });
> });
> 
> /* ====== Global error handler ====== */
> app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
>   console.error(err); // You can hook this into a logger like Winston
>   res.status(err.statusCode || 500).json({
>     error: err.message || 'Internal Server Error',
>   });
> });
> 
> /* ====== Unhandled Rejection guard ====== */
> process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {
>   console.error('Unhandled Rejection at:', promise, 'reason:', reason);
>   // Optional: process.exit(1) if you want to crash gracefully
> });
> 
> /* ====== Start server with graceful shutdown ====== */
> const server = app.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000, () => {
>   console.log(`Server running on port ${server.address().port}`);
> });
> 
> process.on('SIGTERM', () => {
>   console.log('SIGTERM received, shutting down gracefully...');
>   server.close(() => console.log('Process terminated'));
> });
> ```

---

## 4. Operational Advice

| Task | Why ❌ | Fix 💡 |
|------|--------|--------|
| **Use environment variables for secrets** | Hard‑coding a key (or API token) in code is a severe vulnerability. | Store secrets in `.env` (outside the repo) or a vault. |
| **Add a health‑check that verifies DB / external deps** | Raw `/health` return `ok` but doesn't guarantee that downstream services are up. | Inject a lightweight test query to DB or health‑probe. |
| **Log minimal data** | Full request/response bodies can leak secrets. | Log only generic info or masked data. Use a structured logger. |
| **Deploy behind HTTPS** | HTTP opens middle‑man attacks. | Set up TLS termination via a reverse proxy (NGINX/Traefik) or use a cloud provider. |
| **Keep dependencies up‑to‑date** | Vulnerabilities in Express or its utilities. | Run `npm audit` regularly and use `npm audit fix`. |

