### Detailed Rationale for Each Change

| Section | Change | Why it matters (security / resilience) |
|---------|--------|---------------------------------------|
| **`helmet()`** | Adds `XSS` protection, `Content-Type-Options`, `Frame-Options`, etc. | Prevents browser‑based attacks that rely on default header misconfigurations. |
| **`cors()` (with `origin` env var)** | Restricts cross‑origin requests to approved origins. | Mitigates unauthorized front‑end cross‑domain requests. |
| **`rateLimit`** | Limits heavy traffic per IP. | Protects against brute‑force or DoS attacks. |
| **`morgan('combined')`** | Structured request logs. | Makes auditing easier; logs help detect malicious activity. |
| **`async` route handlers + `next(err)`** | Wrap async logic in try/catch and forward errors. | Avoids unhandled promise rejections which crash the server. |
| **Validation with `express-validator`** | Validates any future query/body parameters. | Prevents malformed data and injection attacks. |
| **404 JSON handler** | Provides stable, well‑structured error message. | Forces clients to handle unknown routes correctly. |
| **Global error middleware** | Catches all downstream errors, never leaks stack to prod. | Stops leaking internal details and ensures graceful responses. |
| **Graceful shutdown** | Closes connections on signal. | Avoids abrupt termination that could corrupt state. |
| **`process.env.PORT`** | Configurable port. | Easier deployment to environments (Cloud, Heroku, etc.). |
| **`process.on('unhandledRejection')` / `SIGINT/SIGTERM`** | Additional safety nets. | Ensures server exits cleanly on unrecoverable errors. |

> **Tip for Future Enhancements**  
> If you ever add routes that accept user input (**query strings**, **URL parameters**, **body data**), simply wire them into the existing validation pipeline. For example:

```js
app.get(
  '/users/:id',
  param('id').isInt().withMessage('ID must be an integer'),
  (req, res, next) => {
    // after validation, you can safely parse `req.params.id`
  }
);
```

With these improvements, your `app.js` becomes a **secure, production‑ready** foundation while preserving the same functional API the current test harness expects. Feel free to tweak the configuration values (`CORS_ORIGIN`, `PORT`, rate‑limit limits, etc.) to suit your deployment environment.

