## Step‑by‑step refactor

> **Goal:** 1️⃣ add a handful of lightweight middlewares, 2️⃣ centralise error handling, 3️⃣ enforce environment‑based config.

Below is a single, self‑contained rewrite you can drop into `app.js`.  
If you prefer incremental changes, copy only the blocks you need.

```js
/* ===================== app.js ===================== */
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const cors = require('cors');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
const { json } = require('body-parser');   // defers to express 4.18+
const app = express();

/* ---------- 1️⃣ Environment / config ----------------- */
const PORT = process.env.PORT ?? 3000;
const ENV  = process.env.NODE_ENV ?? 'development';

/* ---------- 2️⃣ Security / performance middlewares ------- */
// 1. Helmet – sets over 20 secure headers (X‑Content‑Type‑Options, etc.)
app.use(helmet());

// 2. CORS – only allow same‑origin or a whitelist
const corsOptions = {
  origin: ENV === 'production' ?
    ['https://mydomain.com'] : // <-- replace with your prod domains
    '*',
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
};
app.use(cors(corsOptions));

// 3. Body parsing – also limits request size
app.use(json({ limit: '1mb' }));

// 4. Rate limiting – prevent abuse of endpoints
app.use(
  rateLimit({
    windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
    max: 200,                 // limit each IP to 200 requests per window
    standardHeaders: true,
    legacyHeaders: false,
  })
);

/* ---------- 3️⃣ Routes --------------------------------- */
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
});

app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  /* 👇 Ideally this data would come from a DB */;
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});

/* ---------- 4️⃣ 404 handler -------------------------------- */
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Endpoint not found' });
});

/* ---------- 5️⃣ Central error handler ---------------------- */
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  /* In production never leak stack traces or sensitive data */
  console.error(err); // still log the full error

  const status = err.status || 500;
  const message = ENV === 'production' ? 'Internal Server Error' : err.message;

  res.status(status).json({
    error: message,
    ...(ENV !== 'production' && { stack: err.stack }),
  });
});

/* ---------- 6️⃣ Start server -------------------------------- */
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running in ${ENV} mode on port ${PORT}`);
});

/* Export for tests – keeps the server start out of test files */
module.exports = app;
```

### What changed & why it matters

| Section | What’s new | Security impact |
|---------|------------|-----------------|
| `helmet()` | Sets headers like `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, etc. | Blocks click‑jacking & MIME sniffing |
| `cors()` | In prod only allow whitelisted origins. | Prevents arbitrary cross‑origin requests that could leak user data |
| Body parser | `limit: '1mb'` + JSON parsing. | Stops huge payloads / JSON‑in‑comment attacks |
| `express-rate-limit` | Cuts off abusive clients. | Defends against brute‑force and DoS |
| 404 handler | JSON error, not plain text. | Avoids leaking the presence/structure of hidden routes |
| Error handler | Centralized; never leaks stack traces in production. | Stops leaking implementation details; logs on the server side |
| `ENV` / `PORT` | Uses env vars. | Makes it safe for deployment, if you forget `NODE_ENV=production`, logs are still verbose for dev |

